WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.580
The Secret Life of Pets might technically
be considered a kids' film, but the story

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.560
is so much fun that it appeals to grown-ups
as well.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:12.300
Of course, watching a movie as an adult isn't
the same experience as watching it as a kid.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.060
Here are things that only adults notice in
The Secret Life of Pets.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:23.240
There's no doubt that Katie loves Max, but
she's not exactly the most responsible pet

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:23.800
parent.

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:26.320
"We met a few years ago.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.720
And boy lemme tell ya, we got along right
away.”

00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:34.900
Early in the story, we can see her biking
with Max who is riding unsecured in a basket

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.360
attached to the front of her bike.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:45.060
"It's me and Katie, Katie and me… us against
the world.”

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.810
When you want to ride a bike with your dog
in real life, they should always be safely

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:50.990
harnessed in the basket.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:55.080
This will keep them secure so they don't bounce
out if you hit a bump in the road.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.620
And speaking of unexpected circumstances,
Katie should obviously be wearing her helmet,

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:00.160
too.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.480
"I know this'll take some getting used to.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.840
But, but I think it's gonna be a great thing
in the long--"

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.560
Duke is a huge dog.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.920
But he's also just an outgoing pupper who
just wants to make friends with his new roommate,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:16.460
Max.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:21.920
He excitedly greets his new canine pal, although
he isn't given the most welcome of greetings

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:22.880
in return.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:28.760
"(Max growl) No Max, ok, it's gonna be alright
Duke.”

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.100
In real life, introducing two dogs to each
other for the first time doesn't always go

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:34.540
that smoothly.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.640
According to the Animal Humane Society, it's
best to keep them leashed at first and allow

00:01:39.649 --> 00:01:41.869
them to slowly get to know each other.

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:45.800
They also should not be left at home alone
together right away, at least not in the same

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:46.360
room.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.800
Katie should have either put them in separate
rooms or crated them, which, at the very least,

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.580
would have prevented Max from creating a mess
in the apartment.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.540
"Welcome to the underbelly brothers.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:02.520
Home of the flushed pets"

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:09.340
The primary antagonists throughout the film
are the Flushed Pets, a motley crew of animals

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.460
that have been abandoned by their owners.

00:02:11.769 --> 00:02:16.540
They make for a well-organized group of militant
animals who are understandably angry with

00:02:16.549 --> 00:02:21.120
humans for how they've been treated — only
accepting Max and Duke after they claim to

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.370
have killed their owners.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:28.580
"Death is comin’ to Brooklyn, and it's got
buck teeth and a cotton tail.”

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:34.260
The group is loosely modeled after the Black
Power movement of the 1960s and '70s, which

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.880
has drawn some criticism since the Flushed
Pets are portrayed as the movie's bad guys,

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.400
and their very real problems are used as comedic
relief.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:48.439
Jason Johnson, a professor of political science
and political editor of The Root wrote that

00:02:48.439 --> 00:02:53.780
the use of racial stereotypes in the film
is problematic and that they quote, "turn

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.180
black protest and death into a joke."

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.480
When Max and Duke go missing, their faithful
friends come to the rescue.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.610
They band together, determined to sniff out
their missing pals and bring them home before

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.560
Katie even realizes they're gone.

00:03:07.989 --> 00:03:12.300
They need some help, though, and one of the
animals they enlist is a predatory hawk who

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.060
lives on the roof of their apartment building.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.240
"I can see for miles.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.140
If you let me out, I'll find your friend.”

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:25.540
But in real life, the city has some pretty
strict laws governing what kind of pets residents

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:29.450
can keep, prohibiting them from owning wild
and illegal animals.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:35.079
On the list of banned pets are not just hawks,
but also other predatory birds such as eagles,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.220
falcons, vultures, and owls.

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:40.780
Like most dogs, Max and Duke love food.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:44.849
When they enter a sausage factory in search
of a snack, they eat so much that they fall

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:49.540
into a food coma and doze off into a daydream
that's basically doggie heaven.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.060
To kids, this scene is a feel good number
with a couple of cute pups.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.060
Adults, though, will recognize that the song
the sausages are singing is from the musical

00:03:59.060 --> 00:03:59.620
Grease.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.360
The iconic number, "We Go Together," is one
that might not be too familiar to kids, but

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:08.140
is sure to strike a chord of nostalgia with
grown-ups.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.310
Co-director, Chris Renaud told Entertainment
Weekly,

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:14.880
"It just felt like [the song] fit perfectly,
truly.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:19.520
It's the kind of scene people either love
or think, what the heck was that?

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.140
I like to think the 'I love it' outweighs
the 'what the heck was that?'

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:23.680
crowd."

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:29.040
"I mean this may be the sausage talking, but
you're ok.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.720
Right back atcha man.”

